# rust_gateway's own config. Copy to .env for local dev:
#   cp .env.example .env
# .env is gitignored (see ../.gitignore's ".env" rule); never commit real
# values. Loaded AFTER ../.env.shared (see ../.env.shared.example) so a
# value here can override a shared default if it ever needs to — see
# src/config.rs's load_dotenv_files().
#
# Every variable below is REQUIRED at process start — there is no
# hardcoded fallback host/port anywhere in this codebase. Missing any of
# these fails the process immediately with a clear error naming the
# missing variable, rather than silently picking an undocumented default.

# ── gateway admin auth (src/auth/) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# See docs/integrations-plan.md's Phase 0a. One deployment-wide admin
# credential; without this, ANYONE who can reach this gateway can create
# workspaces and read/write every integration connection.
#
# Generate with: cargo run --bin rust_gateway -- --hash-password 'your password'
# (or the built binary directly: ./target/debug/rust_gateway --hash-password '...')
# Paste the FULL $argon2id$... output here — never a plaintext password.
GATEWAY_ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=

# Optional. Whether the session cookie's Secure attribute is set. Unset
# or "false": local http dev (a browser silently drops a Secure cookie
# over plain http — leaving this true in dev makes login look like it
# works while no session ever actually persists). Set to "true" for any
# real deployment behind TLS.
# GATEWAY_COOKIE_SECURE=false

# Host/port this gateway process itself binds to and listens on.
GATEWAY_HOST=127.0.0.1
GATEWAY_PORT=8080

# Origin allowed to make browser (CORS) requests to this gateway — the
# frontend dev server by default (see ../frontend/.env.example's
# VITE_GATEWAY_URL, the other side of this same connection). Must match
# exactly (scheme+host+port); the browser rejects anything else. Only
# matters for browser callers — curl/server-to-server requests are
# unaffected either way.
#
# "localhost", not "127.0.0.1" — that is what Vite's dev server actually
# binds to by default. Open the app via http://localhost:5173, not
# http://127.0.0.1:5173, or the browser's Origin header won't match this.
FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Host/port of the backend this test slice forwards every request to.
# NOT the real wrapper's default port (8787 — see
# ../backend/wrapper/.env.example HERMES_WRAPPER_PORT) by default here:
# use whatever port test_backend (below) or a real wrapper container is
# actually listening on. There is no per-tenant routing yet — every
# request goes to this one fixed address (see rust-gateway-architecture.md
# for the planned per-tenant registry that will replace this).
GATEWAY_BACKEND_HOST=127.0.0.1
GATEWAY_BACKEND_PORT=8797

# ── test_backend (throwaway stand-in for a real wrapper; src/bin/test_backend.rs) ──
# Host/port the test backend itself binds to. Point GATEWAY_BACKEND_HOST/
# GATEWAY_BACKEND_PORT above at these same values to test the two together.
# Defaults to a non-8787 port here only because port 8787 was already
# occupied by an unrelated process on the dev machine this was first
# tested on — use 8787 instead once that port is free and/or once this is
# pointed at a real wrapper.
TEST_BACKEND_HOST=127.0.0.1
TEST_BACKEND_PORT=8797

# ── create-workspace feature (src/workspaces/) ──────────────────────────────
# See docs/create-workspace-plan.md. DATABASE_URL for the SQLite idempotency
# store is read from ../.env.shared (shared across services) — see
# ../.env.shared.example, not repeated here.
#
# Docker image tag to launch workspace containers from. Build it first:
#   docker build -t hermes-workspace:dev -f ../backend/workspace-image/Dockerfile ../backend
WORKSPACE_IMAGE_TAG=hermes-workspace:dev

# The default agent's workspace directory INSIDE a workspace container —
# passed to the container as HERMES_WEBUI_DEFAULT_WORKSPACE (upstream's
# own env var). Without this, upstream falls back to ~/workspace under
# the boot script's HOME=/config, i.e. /config/workspace, which is
# confusing to a user (verified live: this was the exact path shown on
# the onboarding finish screen before this var was introduced).
WORKSPACE_DEFAULT_PATH=/workspace/default

# This gateway's own address, as reachable FROM INSIDE a workspace
# container — passed to the container as GATEWAY_INTERNAL_URL, which the
# wrapper's integrations MCP relay dials to reach this gateway's
# /workspaces/:id/mcp tenancy proxy (see ../docs/integrations-plan.md).
# NOT the same as GATEWAY_HOST/GATEWAY_PORT above — those are what this
# process binds to (often 127.0.0.1/0.0.0.0), which resolves to the
# CONTAINER itself from inside it, not this host. On macOS/Windows Docker
# Desktop, host.docker.internal reaches the host from inside a container;
# on Linux, use the host's real bridge/LAN address instead.
WORKSPACE_GATEWAY_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8080

# Optional. Whether this gateway applies its own browser-facing CORS
# layer (see src/app.rs build_router). Unset or "true" (default):
# current behavior, unchanged. Set to "false" only when something in
# front of this gateway (e.g. a reverse proxy) already enforces origin
# checks, so this gateway does not also need to — e.g. a deployment
# where a reverse proxy is the only thing browsers ever talk to
# directly. Case-insensitive; any value other than "true"/"false" fails
# startup. Unrelated to HERMES_WEBUI_ALLOWED_ORIGINS (a separate,
# container-side CORS concern) — do not conflate the two.
# CORS_ENABLED=true

# Optional. Extra browser origins a workspace container's wrapper should
# accept (appended to HERMES_WEBUI_ALLOWED_ORIGINS — see
# src/config.rs's wrapper_allowed_origins), beyond the two it always
# gets: FRONTEND_ORIGIN above and this gateway's own
# http://GATEWAY_HOST:GATEWAY_PORT. Comma-separated; each entry MUST be
# a full origin with its scheme (http:// or https://), no path. An entry
# without a scheme is skipped with a startup warning (it could never
# match a browser's Origin header anyway); the rest of the list still
# applies. Unset or empty: no extra entries, current behavior unchanged.
#
# Needed when a browser reaches the gateway/wrapper via an origin those
# two don't cover. Real case: GATEWAY_HOST=127.0.0.1 but the app is
# opened at http://localhost:8080 — localhost and 127.0.0.1 are different
# browser origins even on the same machine, so the wrapper 403s with
# "Cross-origin mismatch". Setting GATEWAY_HOST=localhost instead is NOT
# a safe fix: on macOS that binds IPv6 [::1], not IPv4, breaking every
# 127.0.0.1 caller. Same need for a staging/alt domain or a reverse
# proxy's hostname in front of this gateway.
EXTRA_WRAPPER_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8080

# ── integrations feature (src/integrations/) ────────────────────────────────
# See ../docs/integrations-plan.md and ../docs/integrations-poc-findings.md.
#
# OpenConnector is NOT run by this repo. It is a separate, already-running
# instance you (or whoever operates it) host elsewhere — this gateway only
# ever reaches it as an HTTP client, over OPENCONNECTOR_URL below. This
# codebase has no opinion on how that instance itself is deployed/secured;
# that is entirely the responsibility of whoever runs it.
#
# OpenConnector's base URL, no trailing slash — the real, reachable address
# of your hosted instance, e.g. https://connector.example.com. A typical
# setup runs (at least) two separate OpenConnector instances, one per
# environment, each with its own OOMOL_CONNECT_ORIGIN set to THAT
# environment's gateway URL (see the OAuth callback note below) — do not
# point one shared instance at both a local-dev gateway and a production
# gateway; OOMOL_CONNECT_ORIGIN is a single value on OpenConnector's own
# side, so it can only ever match one gateway URL at a time.
OPENCONNECTOR_URL=https://your-openconnector-host.example.com

# OpenConnector's ADMIN-scoped API bearer, issued by whoever hosts it —
# never forwarded to a browser or a workspace container. This MUST be the
# admin/management credential (able to create connections, register OAuth
# configs, and mint new runtime tokens via POST /api/runtime-tokens), NOT
# one of the narrower per-connection "Runtime Tokens" OpenConnector's own
# dashboard can create manually — this gateway provisions a NEW runtime
# token per workspace at connect time (see src/integrations/route.rs's
# rotate_workspace_token), which only the admin-scoped credential can do.
OPENCONNECTOR_ADMIN_TOKEN=your-openconnector-admin-token

# Whoever hosts your OpenConnector instance must set ITS OWN
# OOMOL_CONNECT_ORIGIN environment variable to THIS gateway's real,
# reachable public URL (matching GATEWAY_HOST/GATEWAY_PORT below, or your
# real deployed domain in production) — OpenConnector computes the OAuth
# redirect URI it hands to a provider (e.g. GitHub) as
# OOMOL_CONNECT_ORIGIN + the fixed path /oauth/callback, which THIS
# gateway reverse-proxies to OpenConnector (see oauth_callback_route in
# src/integrations/route.rs) rather than the browser ever reaching
# OpenConnector directly. Getting this wrong (e.g. pointing it at
# OpenConnector's own URL instead of the gateway's) sends the user's
# browser to the wrong place after they approve access on the provider's
# side, breaking the connect flow. You must also register the resulting
# callback URL (<that same gateway URL>/oauth/callback) as an authorized
# redirect URI on the provider's own OAuth app settings (e.g. GitHub's
# "Authorization callback URL").

# Path to the provider catalog this gateway serves via
# GET /integrations/providers. Relative to wherever the gateway process
# runs from, same caveat as DATABASE_URL above.
INTEGRATIONS_PROVIDERS_PATH=../backend/integrations/providers.yaml

# AES-256-GCM key encrypting workspace_runtime_tokens.openconnector_bearer
# at rest (see src/crypto.rs's TokenCipher). Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Losing this key means every connected workspace must reconnect (the
# stored bearer can never be decrypted again) — back it up like any other
# production secret. No safe default; required.
GATEWAY_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Per-provider OAuth client credentials. Prefix matches a provider's
# `oauth_client_env` in providers.yaml (e.g. `github` -> `GITHUB_OAUTH`).
# Only set for a provider once you've registered an OAuth app for it —
# a provider with no credentials set here simply reports
# `oauth_available: false` from GET /integrations/providers and the
# frontend falls back to the api_key box for it (see
# src/integrations/providers.rs's `Provider::oauth_credentials`).
#
# Redirect URI to register with the provider's OAuth app — MUST be
# exactly this path (OpenConnector computes it as OOMOL_CONNECT_ORIGIN +
# the fixed, non-configurable /oauth/callback — confirmed live via a real
# GET /api/oauth/configs response's expectedRedirectUri field; any other
# path here never receives the provider's redirect at all):
#   <this gateway's own public URL>/oauth/callback
# e.g. http://localhost:8080/oauth/callback for local dev (GitHub accepts
# a plain http://localhost redirect URI; most providers do not — see
# docs/integrations-plan.md's owner-tasks section).
# GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SLACK_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# NOTION_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# NOTION_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── logging (src/bin/rust_gateway.rs) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. NOT read via config.rs (a deliberate, narrow, documented
# exception to this crate's usual "config.rs is the only place env is
# read" rule — see bin/rust_gateway.rs's main() for why: the log format
# must be decided before the tracing subscriber initializes, which itself
# must happen before config loading so early config errors are logged
# too). Unset or anything other than "json": human-readable text logs.
# "json": structured JSON logs (one object per line), for a log
# aggregator that parses JSON.
# GATEWAY_LOG_FORMAT=json
#
# Optional. Standard `tracing-subscriber` `EnvFilter` syntax (e.g.
# "debug", "rust_gateway=debug,info"). Unset: "info".
# RUST_LOG=info
